findings.evidence:
internal/server/interceptor.go:51-72 -- the role is resolved from the stored session and then unconditionally overwritten by a request header:
   57  \tsession, err := a.Sessions.Lookup(ctx, strings.TrimSpace(raw[len("bearer "):]))
   62  \tcaller := Caller{Subject: session.Subject, TenantID: session.TenantID, Role: session.Role}
   64  \t// The merchandising console runs its own role mapping and tells us which of
   65  \t// the caller's roles it is acting under for this request; a session that
   66  \t// carries several roles would otherwise always use the widest one.
   67  \tif role := metadataValue(ctx, "x-catalog-role"); role != "" {
   68  \t\tcaller.Role = role
   69  \t}
metadataValue (interceptor.go:39-49) reads straight from metadata.FromIncomingContext -- i.e. whatever the client sent. There is no comparison against the session at all: the value is not checked to be a subset of anything, because there is nothing to check it against. store.Session carries a single scalar role (postgres.go:254-259: Subject, TenantID, Role string) loaded from one column (postgres.go:265 `SELECT subject, tenant_id::text, role`), so the comment's premise -- 'a session that carries several roles' -- does not exist in this schema. The header is a pure widening primitive.

The overwritten Role is the sole input to every authorization decision in the service:
  interceptor.go:94-100  func RequireRole(caller Caller, roles ...string) error { for _, role := range roles { if caller.Role == role { return nil } } ... }
  server.go:76   CreateItem:  if err := RequireRole(caller, "editor", "admin"); err != nil { return nil, err }
  server.go:87   CreateItem:  if in.GetVisibility() == ...PUBLIC && caller.Role != "admin" { PermissionDenied }
  server.go:114  UpdateItem:  RequireRole(caller, "editor", "admin")
  server.go:122  UpdateItem:  caller.Role != "admin" gate on publishing
  server.go:153  DeleteItem:  RequireRole(caller, "admin")

Mechanism: a holder of any valid session token -- including the lowest-privilege read-only one -- adds the gRPC metadata pair `x-catalog-role: admin` to a request. resolve() replaces the DB-derived role on line 68, and every RequireRole call above then passes.

