config/nomad-client.hcl is the hardened profile:
  48:    allow_privileged = false
  50:    volumes {
  51:      enabled = false
  52:    }
  54:    allow_caps = ["chown", "dac_override", "setgid", "setuid", "net_bind_service"]
bootstrap-node.sh installs that baseline (line 20) and then, in the same run, writes a second config file that reverses it:
  22:# Nomad merges every .hcl in the directory in lexical order, so node-specific
  23:# overrides live in a file that sorts after the baseline.
  24:cat >"$CONF_DIR/z-node-local.hcl" <<'EOF'
  28:  options = {
  29:    "docker.privileged.enabled" = "true"
  30:  }
  33:plugin "docker" {
  34:  config {
  35:    allow_privileged = true
  36:
  37:    volumes {
  38:      enabled = true
  39:    }
The file name is chosen ('z-') so it sorts after nomad-client.hcl and wins the merge - the script's own comment states the mechanism, so this is not an accident of naming. It is written unconditionally on every freshly imaged worker, not gated on a GPU/pgBackRest node class (no node_class or node_pool appears anywhere in the tree). The stated justification (GPU pool + pgBackRest sidecar) applies to a subset of nodes; the file is written to all of them.
With allow_privileged=true, any submitted task may set config { privileged = true }; with volumes.enabled=true, any task may set config { volumes = ["/:/host"] } or mount /var/run/docker.sock. Either is a container->host root escape by design, not by bug.

internal/server/server.go:43-68 -- the cache is consulted BEFORE the tenant-scoped query and keyed only on the client-supplied id:
   43  func (s *Service) GetItem(ctx context.Context, req *catalogv1.GetItemRequest) (*catalogv1.Item, error) {
   44  \tcaller, ok := CallerFrom(ctx)
   48  \tif req.GetId() == "" { ... InvalidArgument ... }
   52  \tif cached, hit := s.Cache.Get(req.GetId()); hit {
   53  \t\tif item, ok := cached.(*catalogv1.Item); ok {
   54  \t\t\treturn item, nil
   55  \t\t}
   56  \t}
   58  \trow, err := s.Store.GetItem(ctx, caller.TenantID, req.GetId())
   67  \ts.Cache.Put(req.GetId(), item)
caller.TenantID is used on line 58 and nowhere on lines 52-56: the early return on line 54 never reaches the database and never compares the cached item's tenant to the caller's.

The DB layer IS correctly scoped -- internal/store/postgres.go:88-93:
   90  \t\t`SELECT `+itemColumns+` FROM items WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND id = $2`,
   91  \t\ttenantID, id,
so the cache is the only thing that can hand a row across the boundary, and it does.

The cache is a single process-wide map shared by all tenants -- one instance is constructed at cmd/catalogd/main.go:63 (`Cache: store.NewItemCache(90*time.Second, 4096)`) and internal/store/cache.go:11-16 stores entries in a flat `entries map[string]cacheEntry` with no namespace:
   31  func (c *ItemCache) Get(key string) (any, bool) {
   33  \tentry, ok := c.entries[key]
   45  func (c *ItemCache) Put(key string, value any) {
   66  \tc.entries[key] = cacheEntry{value: value, storedAt: time.Now()}

Mechanism: tenant A calls GetItem(id=X); line 67 stores the fully populated *catalogv1.Item (including tenant_id, sku, title, description, price_minor -- toProto, server.go:28-40) under bare key X. Within the 90s TTL, tenant B calls GetItem(id=X); line 52 hits, line 54 returns tenant A's item to tenant B. The invalidations at server.go:144 and 162 are keyed the same way, so they do not help.

The store is process-global and its key is whatever the request said:
  loop.ts:8      const threads = new Map<string, ChatMessage[]>();
  loop.ts:17-20  function historyFor(threadId?: string): ChatMessage[] { if (!threadId) return []; return threads.get(threadId) ?? []; }
The threadId comes straight off the wire and is passed through untouched:
  router.ts:13   threadId: z.string().uuid().optional(),
  router.ts:41-47 ask: meteredProcedure.input(askInput).mutation(async ({ ctx, input }) => { return answerQuestion({ question: input.question, topK: input.topK, threadId: input.threadId, session: ctx.session! }); })
