  loop.ts:34-39  const messages = buildMessages({ question, chunks, history: historyFor(input.threadId), session: input.session });
  prompt.ts:41   return [system, ...input.history, { role: 'user', content: input.question }];
WRITE - the turn is stored back under the same client-chosen key:
  loop.ts:49-55  if (input.threadId) { threads.set(input.threadId, [ ...historyFor(input.threadId), { role: 'user', content: input.question }, { role: 'assistant', content: result.content } ]); }
Every other data path in this component is pinned to ctx.session.workspaceId - weaviate.ts:64 (searchChunks), weaviate.ts:82 (listDocuments), weaviate.ts:109 (deleteDocument), server.ts:44 (the rate-limit bucket). The thread store is the single exception: neither the map key nor any stored record contains workspaceId, subject or the session token, and nothing compares the requesting session against the owner of an existing thread. No code in this tree mints a threadId either - grep for threadId returns only router.ts:13,45 and loop.ts:17-51 - so the value is entirely client-originated.

loop.ts:81-86 (the success branch of the tool dispatch loop):
  81      // Tool output is the authoritative answer to whatever the model asked
  82      // for, so it goes in ahead of the conversation rather than as a reply.
  83      messages.push({
  84        role: 'system',
  85        content: `Result of ${name}:\n${output}`,
  86      });
The comment states the intent explicitly: the output is treated as authoritative and is deliberately not sent back as a reply. Compare the refusal branch fourteen lines earlier, which does use the correct shape:
  loop.ts:69-73   messages.push({ role: 'tool', tool_call_id: call.id, content: `tool ${name} is not available on this plan` });
What lands in that system message for the most-used tool is raw stored document text:
  tools.ts:85-92  case 'search_corpus': { const chunks = await searchChunks({ workspaceId: ctx.session.workspaceId, query: String(args.query ?? ''), topK: Number(args.topK ?? 6) }); return chunks.map((c) => `- ${c.label}: ${c.text}`).join('\n'); }
