There is no post-redirect hostname check, no IP/private-range check, no DNS resolution check, and no custom dispatcher or redirect interceptor anywhere in the tree (crawler.ts:48 is the only call site with maxRedirections; the other three undici calls - tools.ts:95, tools.ts:113, gateway.ts:82 - go to fixed env-configured URLs).
Reachable internal targets are named by the tree's own defaults: http://notify.internal:8070 (tools.ts:6), weaviate.internal:8080 and :50051 (weaviate.ts:12-16), http://model-gateway.internal:8000/v1 (gateway.ts:4), plus link-local metadata endpoints.

CALIBRATION: Researcher proposed high. Downgraded: the initial URL must be admin-supplied AND on the four-host allowlist, so the attacker must control a response on an allowlisted partner documentation host. Real, but not reachable by an arbitrary internet client.

The approval handler resolves the permit by primary key alone and writes the decision with no authorization of any kind:

  29    func approve(req: Request) async throws -> PermitDTO {
  30        guard let permitID = req.parameters.get("permitID", as: UUID.self) else {
  31            throw Abort(.badRequest, reason: "permitID must be a uuid")
  32        }
  33        guard let permit = try await Permit.find(permitID, on: req.db) else {
  34            throw Abort(.notFound)
  35        }
  36        let body = try req.content.decode(ApprovalRequest.self)
  37        guard ["approved", "refused", "withdrawn"].contains(body.decision) else {
  38            throw Abort(.badRequest, reason: "unknown decision")
  39        }
  40        permit.status = body.decision
  41        permit.reviewerEmail = body.reviewerEmail
  42        permit.internalReviewNotes = body.notes
  43        try await permit.save(on: req.db)

`Permit.find(permitID, ...)` at line 33 is unscoped - compare PermitController.detail/update/attach, which all chain `.filter(\.$tenantID == user.tenantID)` (PermitController.swift:80, 114, 137). And the role that exists for exactly this decision is dead code: `var isApprover: Bool { roles.contains("approver") || roles.contains("supervisor") }` at Middleware/BearerAuthMiddleware.swift:12 has zero references in the tree (verified by grep for `isApprover`). Roles are loaded from the DB on every request (BearerAuthMiddleware.swift:49, 60) and then never used to gate anything.

reviewerEmail and notes are taken verbatim from the request body (lines 41-42) - the caller writes whose signature appears on the decision and what the internal review record says.

Ingress: POST /internal/permits/<permitID>/approve, registered at AdminController.swift:23; the permitID comes from the URL at line 30 and the body is decoded at line 36. As the tree stands this route carries no authentication either (routes.swift:45-46, reported separately), so it is reachable with no credential at all; I rate this finding medium rather than high because that unauthenticated exposure is counted in the separate finding. As an independent defect it survives that fix: once BearerAuthMiddleware is attached, any staff user of any tenant, with no approver or supervisor role, still decides any permit in any council.

